Chapter: BoundedExceeded
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Cost Model 3.3. `bounded <class> <name>` declares a CEILING in the lattice
 `none < fixed < linear < growing`, and this chapter is the refusal arm: a
 function that declares `bounded linear` and is inferred `growing`.

 The refusal must be TRANSITIVE, which is the half a self-contained arm would
 miss. `walks` is linear in its own body -- it calls a helper and returns what
 the helper returns -- and it is refused because `grow-loop` copies its
 accumulator inside a self call. That is the `unpack-text` shape the whole
 feature exists for: linear where you read it, quadratic underneath.

 The right operand must ALLOCATE, and this arm was rebuilt once because it
 did not. It appended the literal `"x"`, and COMPILER-8 (main 16039) made a
 Text accumulator extend in place while it stays topmost, so that function
 became genuinely linear (`p-append-text`, x3.6 in
 `codex/test/cost/accumulator-corpus`) and the declaration it was supposed to
 break started holding. `show i` allocates BETWEEN the appends and pushes the
 accumulator off the frontier, which is what still copies (`p-append-show`,
 x15.5). Anything put here must be a shape the corpus measures quadratic.

Section: The Callee

  grow-loop : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  grow-loop (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else grow-loop n (i + 1) (acc & show i)

Section: The Declaration That Cannot Hold

  bounded linear walks : Integer -> Text
  walks (n) = grow-loop n 0 ""

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (walks 4)
  end
